From d4f62f96d967429220ecdbada57737cb16f58edc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jakub Jelinek <jakub@redhat.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 10:47:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] tailc: Conservatively punt on some return type mismatches in
 tail call opt [PR127848]

The first testcase is miscompiled on x86_64 and various other
bitint_ext_undef targets, where the padding bits are unspecified.
So, if callee returns unsigned _BitInt(15), only low 15 bits are
specified, when caller returns unsigned short on x86_64, the bits 16-63
are unspecified, but bit 15 needs to be cleared, so we can't tail
call.

Now, tailc for casts currently checks TYPE_MODE differences and for
the destination doesn't have non-mode precision (that one was added
because of callee returning unsigned char and caller returning bool,
those have the same mode but need to emit some code in between the
call to the callee and return from caller).
For useless_type_conversion_p we keep the distinction between normal
INTEGER_TYPEs and BITINT_TYPE_P types with the same precision, because
at function boundaries the target can have different calling convention.
We don't really know at GIMPLE if the target returns unsigned _BitInt(16)
and unsigned short the same way, there could be e.g. differences in padding
bits etc.
The following patch is a conservative fix, it punts on
BITINT_TYPE_P != BITINT_TYPE_P conversions (exactly because we don't
know if it changes ABI or not), and if both are BITINT_TYPE_P with the
same TYPE_MODE, does some further checks - if TYPE_SIZE is different
(and mode the same, that could be just BLKmode) it punts on TYPE_SIZE
differences, for narrowing or same precision casts it punts except
for bitint_ext_undef targets (on those the padding bits are unspecified
and if we have more or same amount of unspecified padding bits it is ok),
for widening casts it punts on the other side for all but bitint_ext_full
targets (I'm not motivated enough to bother with figuring out all the
details for bitint_ext_partial), because on those targets the narrower
bitint has the bits already extended and if the narrower and wider _BitInt
type have the same signedness, they are fine (now that I think about it,
perhaps we could allow even narrower unsigned wider signed, can test
that incrementally).
In any case, the patch may pessimize code which would work just fine,
callee returning unsigned _BitInt(16) and caller unsigned short or vice
versa on many targets, etc.  But, there can be multiple casts, so if we
want to improve it, we could e.g. have casts where the callee type is
compatible with the src_var type and dest type is compatible with the
caller's type (so have these checks for intermediate calls but allow
one cast from callee to caller return types), and then somehow deal with it
during expansion (either in generic code try to expand code which converts
the callee type temporary to caller type and check (and throw away) a
sequence emitted by that whether it contains only normal moves and no other
insns, or make sure every target verifies everything in their target hook).
I don't have time to do that right now though and it might be too risky
for backports anyway.

2026-10-13  Jakub Jelinek  <jakub@redhat.com>

	PR middle-end/127848
	* tree-tailcall.cc: Include target.h.
	(process_assignment): Punt if one of dest and src_var types
	is BITINT_TYPE_P and the other is not.  If both are BITINT_TYPE_P,
	punt if TYPE_SIZE is different, or for narrowing or same precision
	conversions on != bitint_ext_undef targets, or for widening
	conversions on != bitint_ext_full targets or if signedness differs.

	* gcc.dg/pr127848-1.c: New test.
	* gcc.dg/pr127848-2.c: New test.
	* gcc.dg/pr127848-3.c: New test.
	* gcc.dg/pr127848-4.c: New test.
	* gcc.dg/pr127848-5.c: New test.

Reviewed-by: Richard Biener <rguenth@suse.de>
(cherry picked from commit 08c26561e4fe2cb6a26f5217673ed72c22d33e5b)
